In Java, comparing strings is done differently than comparing primitive data types like integers or booleans. The == operator checks if two objects refer to the same memory location, rather than checking their content. This means that when you use == on strings, you are actually checking if they are the exact same object, not if they have the same value.

The equals() method, on the other hand, compares the values of two strings character by character. It checks if the contents of both strings are equal.

Here's an example to illustrate this:
```java
String str1 = new String("Hello");
String str2 = "Hello";

System.out.println(str1 == str2); // Output: false
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // Output: true
```
In this case, str1 and str2 are not the same object (even though they have the same value), hence the == comparison returns false. But when we use equals(), it correctly identifies that the values of the two strings are the same.

So, when should you use == and when should you use equals()?

Use == when you want to check if two variables point to the exact same object. This is useful in cases where you create a string object once and reuse it multiple times. For example:
```java
String constantString = "SomeConstantValue";
// ... some code ...
if (constantString == anotherVariable) {
    // They are the same object
}
```
Use equals() when you want to check if the content of two strings is the same, regardless of whether they are the same object or not. This is the most common scenario and how you should compare strings in general:
```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";
// ... some code ...
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    // Their content is the same
}
```
In summary, == is used for reference equality (checking if two variables point to the same object), while equals() is used for value equality (checking if the content of two objects is the same).

